# Task description

You are a coding assistent. We will write for a Django backend together using the following steps:

- step 1: module specification;
- step 2: scenario description;
- step 3: code generation guided by forms and code-snippets.

Below I will describe each step in more detail, and I will provide you with examples. These examples
are based on the fictional use-case of adding a new blog-post to a blog.

## Explanation of Step 1: module specification

In this step I will give you a high-level description of the Django app modules that will be created.
This description contains a module-specification for every module in the application.
The comments in the module-specification that are specifically for your information are
marked with `SPEC: ...`.

Here are the example module-specifications for the BlogsModule and BlogPostsModule:

```python
class Blogs(Module):
    # SPEC: This section describes all django models in the module.
    class Models:
        class Blog(Entity):
            """A Django model that represents a blog"""

            id: UUID
            name: str
            description: str
            blog_posts: List[BlogPosts.Models.BlogPost]

    # SPEC: An action is a function in the module that updates the application state.
    class Actions:
        class CreateBlog(Action):
            class Args:
                create_blog_form: Form

    class ApiViews:
        class CreateBlogApiView(ApiView):
            pass

class BlogPosts(Module):
    class Models:
        class BlogPost(Entity):
            """A Django model that contains a blog-post"""

            id: UUID
            blog_id: UUID
            name: str
            content: str

    class Actions:
        class CreateBlogPost(Action):
            class Args:
                create_blog_post_form: Form
                blog: Blogs.Models.Blog

    class ApiViews:
        class CreateBlogPostApiView(ApiView):
            pass
```

## Explanation of Step 2: scenario description

In this step I will describe a scenario that must be supported by the backend. I will mention actors (i.e. the
objects in the scenario) that are based on the module-specification. The actors are described using a special
notation: [(actor name+)(actor sub-type):actor-type]. For example:

- the notation `hello-world+:blog-post` means that the type is `blog-post` and the name is `hello-world`.
- the notation `wow+special:blog-post` means that the type is `special:blog-post` and the name is `wow`.

Here are two example scenario-descriptions. In the first one, the user creates a new blog. In this scenario,
the api-view only calls one action, but in other scenarios it might call multiple actions.

```markdown
# Scenario: a user creates a blog

## The [:frontend] calls the [create-blog:api-view]

### The [create-blog:api-view] calls the [create-blog:action] to create a [my-blog+:blog]

The following actors are created in this step:

[my-blog+:blog]:
id=1
name=my blog
description=My personal blog
blog_posts=[]

#### The [create-blog-post:action] validates the name of the [my-blog+:blog-post]
```

In the second scenario, the user creates a blog-post. Note that this scenario doesn't mention a validation
step, but when we generate code together in the future then you will be asked to consider the validations that
are necessary.

```markdown
# Scenario: a user creates a blog-post

## The [:frontend] calls the [create-blog-post:api-view]

### The [create-blog-post:api-view] calls the [create-blog-post:action] to create a [hello-world+:blog-post]

The following actors are created in this step:

[hello-world+:blog-post]:
name=hello world
content=Hello world, this is my first blog post
blog_id=1

The following actors are updated in this step:

[my-blog+:blog]:
blog_posts=[hello-world+:blog-post]
```

## Explanation of step 3: Code generation

In this step I will present you with some building blocks.
Each building block has a form and a code-snippet that utilizes the information in the form.
When we generate actual code later then I will ask you to map the information in the module specification
and the scenario description onto the form. The form is then used to generate the code by closely following
the code structure of the corresponding code snippet.

I will now show you the first building block so that you can understand the approach.

### The action building-block

The action building-block is used to create an action function.
Here is the form. The example values in this form correspond to BlogPosts.Actions.CreateBlogPost.
When we use this form in a real-life situation then I will ask you to fill out the form step-by-step,
so that we can focus on each concern separately.

